/// The closed-world judgement over the typed family of generator.ft: a function that the compiler
/// accepts with exit 0 gives no error event when the oracle of fir.interpreter runs it, and no
/// run of it ends in a fault that no event explains (spec/ownership-design.md 3.3, 3.4).
//
// A batch is the text of one entry module: the declarations of one cell and up to BATCH functions
// of it. The judgement checks the batch, lowers each function, and gives each function a verdict
// as a build with `--whole-store-report --leak-report --span-del-report` does: the passes of
// fort.owner.prover, the report lines of fort.owner.report, the release-only bit of each drop of a
// `del(s, d)` (D17.9), and the `del` of a span of owners with one operand. Then it runs each
// function that the checker and the lowering take, with each input that the function reads, and
// counts the events of the run by kind.
//
// The compiler gives exit 0 for a function with the verdict `accepted` or `reported`, and the
// judgement covers both. Each event of a run of such a function gets a ruling (rule). The rule
// reads the one disposition table of the event kinds (test/fort/support/disposition.ft). The gate
// of the oracle tests (test/fort/owner/oracle_test.ft) takes the ruling of each event of its items
// from the same function (ruling_of). So one rule judges the hand items, the seeded family, the
// shapes and the typed family.
//
// The rule takes two events as no error. A `leak` at a `dead` marker of a block that ends in an
// abort has an excuse, because a path that ends in a runtime error keeps its memory (D17.20). A
// `leak at exit` of a cycle of owners is defined behavior. The table trusts six kinds: such an
// event needs a program that breaks an assumption of the rules. The rule takes an event of such
// a kind as an error, because the compiler must not accept that program. So each of the ten
// event kinds is an error. A report option owns an event kind, and a line of that option that
// names the line of the event takes the error: `--leak-report` owns `leak`,
// `--whole-store-report` owns `overwrite`, and `--span-del-report` owns the `leak` on the line of
// a `del` that it lists. A report line takes no event on another line of its function, and a
// line of a pass takes no event of another function. No report option owns another kind, so a
// report line does not take a `leak at exit`, a `use after move` or a `use after release`.
//
// A function with an error event is a failure, and so is a function with a run that ends in a
// fault other than `null dereference` with no error event. The judgement keeps one record for a
// failure, with what its first error is, the place of it, and each kind of error of its runs.
// Each failure has a class of CLASSES or no class (class_of). A class is a known cause, which a
// later change of the compiler or of the run time removes. A failure with no class is a new one.
// KNOWN is the one list of the known failures: the count of the failures of each class of each
// unit, for the three sets of the gate and for each part of the typed family (holds_known).
import check_env;
import disposition;
import fort.ast;
import fort.check;
import fort.diag;
import fort.fir;
import fort.fir.interpreter;
import fort.fir.release;
import fort.fir.shadow;
import fort.fir_lower;
import fort.fir_verify;
import fort.owner.prover;
import fort.owner.report;
import fort.types;
import generator;
import modules_env;
import run_env;
import std.str;
import std.strbuf;

// No item.
u64 NONE = 18446744073709551615;

// The most rounds of prepare.
u64 ROUNDS = 8;

/// The most functions of a batch.
u64 BATCH = 200;

// The first line of the `dead` markers of a batch after relocate. No text of a batch has as many
// lines.
u32 DEAD_LINE = 1000000;

/// The file name of the entry module of a batch.
string ENTRY = "main.ft";

/// Whether the path `file` names the entry module: its last component is ENTRY. A path that ends
/// in ENTRY after another character than `/`, such as `xmain.ft`, names another file.
fn is_entry(string file) bool {
    if (!str.ends_with(file, ENTRY)) {
        return false;
    }
    return file.len == ENTRY.len || file[file.len - ENTRY.len - 1] == '/';
}

/// What the compiler says about a function, in the order of VERDICT_TEXT: it accepts the function
/// with no report line; it accepts it with a report line; a pass of the prover or the release-only
/// rule gives an error; or the checker, the lowering or the verifier refuses it. A function takes
/// the last verdict of this order that holds. The compiler gives exit 0 for the first two.
enum verdict {
    vd_accepted,
    vd_reported,
    vd_rejected,
    vd_refused,
}

/// The count of the members of verdict.
u64 VERDICTS = 4;
string[VERDICTS] VERDICT_TEXT = {"accepted", "reported", "rejected", "refused"};

/// The report options that own an event kind, as bits: `--leak-report`, `--whole-store-report`
/// and `--span-del-report`.
u32 REPORT_LEAK = 1;
u32 REPORT_WHOLE = 2;
u32 REPORT_SPAN = 4;

/// The count of the report options that own an event kind, and the name of each in the order of
/// its bit.
u64 REPORTS = 3;
string[REPORTS] REPORT_TEXT = {"leak report", "whole store report", "span del report"};

/// A line of the text of a batch that a line of a report option names, and the REPORT bit of
/// that option. `of` is the function of a line that a pass of the prover reports. It is null for
/// a line of `--span-del-report`, which the checker reports with no function.
struct named_line {
    u32 bit;
    u32 line;
    ast.sym* of;
}

/// The lines that the report lines of one batch name, in the order of the report (push_named).
struct named_vec {
    named_line mut@ own items;
    u64 len;
}

/// What the judgement says about an event of a run of a function with exit 0, in the order of
/// RULING_TEXT: it is an error; it has the excuse of an abort; a report option owns it; or it is
/// the `leak at exit` of a cycle of owners.
enum ruling {
    ru_error,
    ru_excused,
    ru_leak_report,
    ru_whole_report,
    ru_span_report,
    ru_cycle,
}

/// The count of the members of ruling.
u64 RULINGS = 6;
string[RULINGS] RULING_TEXT = {
    "error",
    "excused at an abort",
    "leak report",
    "whole store report",
    "span del report",
    "leak of a cycle",
};

/// The count of the members of shadow.event_kind.
u64 KINDS = 10;

/// How a run ended, in the order of END_TEXT: the members of interpreter.outcome_kind, with a
/// fault as one of three rows.
u64 ENDS = 9;
u64 END_NULL = 6;
u64 END_ACCESS = 7;
u64 END_FAULT = 8;
string[ENDS] END_TEXT = {
    "none", "return", "exit", "abort", "trap", "outside", "null dereference", "invalid access",
    "other fault",
};

/// What the first error of a failure is: a member of shadow.event_kind, or a run that ends in
/// `invalid access` or in another fault with no error event.
u64 WHAT_ACCESS = KINDS;
u64 WHAT_FAULT = KINDS + 1;

/// Where the first error of a failure stands, when it stands on no statement of the function: on
/// another line of the function, at a `dead` marker of the function, which ends a scope, or in a
/// declaration of the batch. A statement gives its row of generator.OPERATIONS.
u64 AT_END = generator.OPERATION_COUNT;
u64 AT_SCOPE = generator.OPERATION_COUNT + 1;
u64 AT_CALLEE = generator.OPERATION_COUNT + 2;

/// The count of the places of an error.
u64 PLACES = generator.OPERATION_COUNT + 3;
string[3] PLACE_TEXT = {"the end", "a scope end", "a callee"};

/// A failure: function `seq` of `n` operations of the cell of row `ty` of generator.TYPES and row
/// `st` of generator.STORAGES, its verdict, what its first error is, and the place of that error.
/// `ops` holds the row of generator.OPERATIONS of each statement, and `pos` is the statement of
/// the error, or `n` when the error stands on no statement. `kinds` holds one bit for each kind
/// of error of the runs of the function: bit `what` for the error `what`.
struct failure {
    u64 ty;
    u64 st;
    u64 n;
    u64 seq;
    verdict said;
    u64 what;
    u64 at;
    u64 pos;
    u64 kinds;
    u64[generator.MOST_STATEMENTS] ops;
}

struct failure_vec {
    failure mut@ own items;
    u64 len;
}

/// The counts of the judgement. `verdicts` counts the functions by verdict, `runs` each run,
/// `events` each event of each run by kind, and `named` the lines that the report lines of each
/// batch name, by option, with each repeat. The other counts are of the runs of the functions
/// with exit 0: `judged` counts those runs and `ends` counts them by their end, `rulings` counts
/// their events by ruling, `errors` their error events by kind, and `silent` the runs that end in
/// a fault other than `null dereference` with no error event. `failures` holds one record for
/// each failure. A value lives in an allocation of make, so each step takes it by pointer.
struct totals {
    u64 functions;
    u64[VERDICTS] verdicts;
    u64 runs;
    u64 judged;
    u64[ENDS] ends;
    u64[KINDS] events;
    u64[KINDS] errors;
    u64[RULINGS] rulings;
    u64[REPORTS] named;
    u64 silent;
    failure_vec failures;
}

/// Empty counts in an allocation of their own. It stops when the disposition table does not name
/// each event kind in the order of fir.shadow (disposition.is_complete): print_totals reads row
/// `kind` of that table for the kind at position `kind`.
fn make() totals mut* own {
    if (!disposition.is_complete()) {
        panic("judgement.make: the table of disposition.ft does not name each event kind");
    }
    return new(totals);
}

/// Ends counts of make that the caller moves in.
fn drop_made(totals mut* own p) void {
    totals kept = move(*p);
    del(p);
    del(kept.failures.items);
}

// A function of a batch: its index in the cell, its lines in the text, its verdict, whether it
// is a failure and the position of its record in the failures of the counts, its symbol, and what
// generator.write_typed says about it.
struct item {
    u64 seq;
    u32 first;
    u32 last;
    verdict said;
    bool failed;
    u64 record;
    ast.sym* sym;
    generator.typed_shape shape;
}

// One batch: the cell, the count `n` of the operations of each function, the vocabulary of the
// cell, the items, the text, the functions as the lowering makes them, for each `dead` marker
// whether its block ends in an abort, and `named`: each line that a line of a report option
// names (name_reports, prove_batch). A batch of hand_make has no cell: `reads` holds the inputs
// that each of its functions reads.
struct batch {
    u64 ty;
    u64 st;
    u64 n;
    u64[generator.OPERATION_COUNT] ops;
    u64 size;
    u32 reads;
    item mut@ own items;
    strbuf.str_buf text;
    fir.module m;
    bool mut@ own aborts;
    u64 deads;
    named_vec named;
}

// A batch of `count` functions of `n` operations of the cell, from function `first`, in an
// allocation of its own: each step takes the batch by pointer.
fn batch_make(u64 ty, u64 st, u64 n, u64 first, u64 count) batch mut* own {
    batch mut* own bt = new(batch);
    bt->ty = ty;
    bt->st = st;
    bt->n = n;
    bt->items = new(item, count);
    bt->size = generator.typed_vocabulary(ty, st, bt->ops[..]);
    for (u64 mut k = 0; k < count; k++) {
        bt->items[k].seq = first + k;
    }
    return bt;
}

// A batch of `count` functions that the caller writes, each of which reads the inputs `reads`.
fn hand_make(u64 count, u32 reads) batch mut* own {
    batch mut* own bt = new(batch);
    bt->reads = reads;
    bt->items = new(item, count);
    for (u64 mut k = 0; k < count; k++) {
        bt->items[k].seq = k;
    }
    return bt;
}

// Frees a batch that the caller moves in.
fn batch_drop(batch mut bt) void {
    del(bt.items);
    strbuf.drop(move(bt.text));
    fir.module_drop(move(bt.m));
    del(bt.aborts);
    del(bt.named.items);
}

// Ends a batch of batch_make that the caller moves in: the value moves out before the `del` of the
// allocation, and batch_drop frees it.
fn batch_drop_made(batch mut* own p) void {
    batch kept = move(*p);
    del(p);
    batch_drop(move(kept));
}

// Whether `s` is a function of the entry module of the batch.
fn is_in_main(ast.sym* s) bool {
    return s->owner != null && s->owner->name == "main";
}

// Advances *line over the newlines of `b` after *seen.
fn count_lines(strbuf.str_buf* b, u64 mut* seen, u32 mut* line) void {
    string text = strbuf.view(b);
    for (u64 mut i = *seen; i < text.len; i++) {
        if (text[i] == '\n') {
            *line += 1;
        }
    }
    *seen = text.len;
}

// Writes the text of `bt` with each item that the checker and the lowering take, and the lines of
// each such item. The functions declare and call `oracle_keep` and its kin, so the text is trusted
// (D9.8). A batch of hand_make has the declarations `decls` and the functions `texts`, one for
// each item; each other batch has the empty `texts`.
fn write_batch(batch mut* bt, string decls, string@ texts) void {
    strbuf.str_buf mut* b = &bt->text;
    strbuf.clear(b);
    strbuf.append(b, check_env.TRUST);
    if (texts.len > 0) {
        strbuf.append(b, decls);
    } else {
        generator.write_typed_prelude(b, bt->ty, bt->st);
    }
    u64 mut seen = 0;
    u32 mut line = 1;
    count_lines(b, &seen, &line);
    for (u64 mut k = 0; k < bt->items.len; k++) {
        item mut* it = &bt->items[k];
        if (it->said == verdict.vd_refused) {
            continue;
        }
        it->first = line;
        if (texts.len > 0) {
            strbuf.append(b, texts[k]);
            it->shape.reads = bt->reads;
            it->shape.first = 1;
        } else {
            generator.write_typed(b, bt->ty, bt->st, bt->ops[..bt->size], bt->n, it->seq, it->seq,
                                  &it->shape);
        }
        count_lines(b, &seen, &line);
        it->last = line - 1;
    }
}

// The position in `bt->items` of the item whose lines hold `line`, or NONE.
fn item_at(batch* bt, u32 line) u64 {
    for (u64 mut k = 0; k < bt->items.len; k++) {
        item* it = &bt->items[k];
        if (it->said != verdict.vd_refused && it->first <= line && line <= it->last) {
            return k;
        }
    }
    return NONE;
}

// Gives the item whose lines hold `line` the verdict `said`, when its verdict comes before `said`
// in the order of verdict. A line of no item is a declaration of the batch, which the compiler
// must accept: the test stops there.
fn mark(batch mut* bt, u32 line, verdict said) void {
    u64 k = item_at(bt, line);
    if (k == NONE) {
        println(strbuf.view(&bt->text));
        panic("judgement.mark: the compiler does not accept a declaration of the batch");
    }
    if (cast(bt->items[k].said, i32) < cast(said, i32)) {
        bt->items[k].said = said;
    }
}

// Checks the text of `bt` in a fresh sandbox, and gives whether it checks with no diagnostic. Each
// item whose lines hold an error becomes refused.
fn check_batch(check_env.env mut* c, batch mut* bt) bool {
    check_env.reopen(c);
    run_env.add_std(c, run_env.RUNTIME[..]);
    modules_env.std_dir_without_runtime(&c->m, "std");
    c->want_main = false;
    if (check_env.check_src(c, strbuf.view(&bt->text))) {
        return true;
    }
    bool mut refused = false;
    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < modules_env.reports(&c->m); i++) {
        diag.record r = modules_env.report_at(&c->m, i);
        if (r.severity == diag.severity.error && is_entry(r.loc.file)
            && item_at(bt, r.loc.line) != NONE) {
            mark(bt, r.loc.line, verdict.vd_refused);
            refused = true;
        }
    }
    if (!refused) {
        eprintln(check_env.report_text(c, 0));
        panic("judgement.check_batch: a declaration of the batch does not check");
    }
    return false;
}

// Lowers the function `s` into `bt->m`, and gives whether the lowering and the verifier accept
// it. A refused function makes its item refused. The function reads `s` before it lends its own
// FIR function, so the use rule of `move` and `del` holds each read.
fn lower_one(check_env.env mut* c, batch mut* bt, ast.sym* s, fir_verify.runtime* rt) bool {
    ast.node* body = s->node;
    u32 line = s->decl.line;
    bool in_main = is_in_main(s);
    fir.func mut* own f = fir.func_make();
    // an accepted function moves out into the module, and the drop ends the empty value
    defer fir.func_drop_made(move(f));
    fir_lower.report mut why = fir_lower.report{};
    bool lowered = fir_lower.lower_function(&c->ck, body, f, &why);
    fir_verify.violation mut v = fir_verify.violation{};
    if (lowered && !fir_verify.find_violation(f, rt, &v)) {
        fir.module_add_function(&bt->m, move(*f));
        return true;
    }
    if (!in_main || item_at(bt, line) == NONE) {
        panic("judgement.lower_one: the lowering or the verifier refuses a declaration");
    }
    mark(bt, line, verdict.vd_refused);
    return false;
}

// Lowers each function with a body of the closure into `bt->m`, and gives whether the lowering
// and the verifier accept each.
fn lower_batch(check_env.env mut* c, batch mut* bt) bool {
    fir_verify.runtime rt = fir_verify.runtime_of(&c->ck);
    bool mut accepted = true;
    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < check.check_sym_count(&c->ck); i++) {
        ast.sym* s = check.check_sym_at(&c->ck, i);
        if (s->kind == ast.sym_kind.s_fn && s->node != null && s->node->b != null
            && !lower_one(c, bt, s, &rt)) {
            accepted = false;
        }
    }
    return accepted;
}

// Writes, checks and lowers `bt` until each item that is not refused checks, lowers and verifies.
// `decls` and `texts` are those of write_batch.
fn prepare(check_env.env mut* c, batch mut* bt, string decls, string@ texts) void {
    for (u64 mut round = 0; round < ROUNDS; round++) {
        fir.module_free(&bt->m);
        bt->m = fir.module_new();
        write_batch(bt, decls, texts);
        if (check_batch(c, bt) && lower_batch(c, bt)) {
            return;
        }
    }
    panic("judgement.prepare: the batch does not settle");
}

// Gives the item of the function `f` its symbol and the verdict of the proof `p`: rejected after
// an error of a pass, and reported when fort.owner.report writes a line for `--leak-report` or
// `--whole-store-report`. A text of a hand batch holds one function: when a text holds two, each
// gives the item its verdict, and the last one gives it the symbol, so only the last one runs.
fn prove_one(check_env.env mut* c, batch mut* bt, fir.func* f) void {
    u32 line = f->sym->decl.line;
    u64 k = item_at(bt, line);
    prover.proof mut* own p = prover.prove_made(f);
    defer prover.drop_made(move(p));
    bool reported = name_reports(c, &bt->named, f, p) != 0;
    if (k != NONE) {
        bt->items[k].sym = f->sym;
    }
    if (prover.has_error(p)) {
        mark(bt, line, verdict.vd_rejected);
    } else if (reported) {
        mark(bt, line, verdict.vd_reported);
    }
}

/// Appends `add` to `v`.
fn push_named(named_vec mut* v, named_line add) void {
    if (v->len == v->items.len) {
        named_line mut@ own bigger = new(named_line,
                                         fir.grown_capacity(v->len, "judgement: capacity"));
        for (u64 mut i = 0; i < v->len; i++) {
            bigger[i] = v->items[i];
        }
        del(v->items);
        v->items = move(bigger);
    }
    v->items[v->len] = add;
    v->len += 1;
}

/// The line that the line `text` of a report names, as fort.owner.report writes it at the start:
/// `<file>:<line>:<col>: `. It gives 0, which is no line, when `text` has another shape or the
/// file is not the entry module (is_entry).
fn named_of(string text) u32 {
    i64 colon = str.index_of(text, ':');
    if (colon < 0 || !is_entry(text[..cast(colon, u64)])) {
        return 0;
    }
    string rest = text[cast(colon, u64) + 1..];
    i64 end = str.index_of(rest, ':');
    u64 mut line = 0;
    if (end < 0 || !str.parse_u64(rest[..cast(end, u64)], 10, &line)
        || line >= cast(DEAD_LINE, u64)) {
        return 0;
    }
    return cast(line, u32);
}

/// The end of a line of `--leak-report` that stands at a `cast` (fort.owner.report).
string CAST_END = "before this cast";

/// Whether the type `t` holds an owner in its fixed storage: an `own` reference, or an owning
/// aggregate (D17.7), from the mark of the checker.
fn holds_owners(types.node* t) bool {
    return (types.type_is_reference(t) && t->is_own) || types.type_is_owning_aggregate(t);
}

/// Whether the statement `st` of `f` is a `del` of an `own` pointer to an object that holds no
/// owner by its type: a `void` pointer, or a pointer to a type with no owner. Such a `del` gives a
/// `leak` only after a `cast` that carried `own` there from a pointer to an object with owners,
/// and the pass reports that `cast` (D17.20, part 2).
fn is_bare_del(fir.func* f, fir.statement* st) bool {
    if (st->kind != fir.stmt_kind.st_del) {
        return false;
    }
    types.node* t = fir.place_type(f, st->place);
    if (t->kind == types.type_kind.k_voidptr) {
        return t->is_own;
    }
    return t->kind == types.type_kind.k_ptr && t->is_own && !holds_owners(t->elem);
}

/// The count of the `cast` statements of `f`: each statement that assigns the value of a `cast`.
fn cast_count(fir.func* f) u64 {
    u64 mut count = 0;
    for (u32 mut b = 0; b < fir.block_count(f); b++) {
        fir.block* bl = fir.block_at(f, b);
        for (u64 mut k = 0; k < bl->statements.len; k++) {
            fir.statement* st = &bl->statements.items[k];
            if (st->kind == fir.stmt_kind.st_assign && st->value.kind == fir.rvalue_kind.rv_cast) {
                count += 1;
            }
        }
    }
    return count;
}

// Whether `p` is a local of `f` with no projection.
fn is_whole_local(fir.place p) bool {
    return p.base == fir.base_kind.bk_local && p.proj_len == 0;
}

// Whether the statement `st` of `f` gives a whole local the result of a `cast`, or a `move` or a
// copy of a whole local that `made` marks.
fn makes_cast(fir.func* f, fir.statement* st, bool@ made) bool {
    if (st->kind != fir.stmt_kind.st_assign || !is_whole_local(st->place)) {
        return false;
    } else if (st->value.kind == fir.rvalue_kind.rv_cast) {
        return true;
    } else if (st->value.kind != fir.rvalue_kind.rv_use) {
        return false;
    }
    fir.operand o = fir.rvalue_operand(f, st->value, 0);
    return o.kind != fir.operand_kind.ok_const && is_whole_local(o.place) && made[o.place.local];
}

// Marks in `made` each local of `f` that holds the result of a `cast` of `f`: a `cast` assigns it,
// or a `move` or a copy of such a local. The marks grow until no statement adds one.
fn mark_cast_made(fir.func* f, bool mut@ made) void {
    bool mut grew = true;
    while (grew) {
        grew = false;
        for (u32 mut b = 0; b < fir.block_count(f); b++) {
            fir.block* bl = fir.block_at(f, b);
            for (u64 mut k = 0; k < bl->statements.len; k++) {
                fir.statement* st = &bl->statements.items[k];
                if (makes_cast(f, st, made) && !made[st->place.local]) {
                    made[st->place.local] = true;
                    grew = true;
                }
            }
        }
    }
}

// Records the line of each `del` of `f` that is_bare_del names and whose operand is a whole local
// that holds the result of a `cast` of `f` (mark_cast_made), for the option REPORT_LEAK: a line of
// `--leak-report` at the one `cast` of `f` names them too. The owners of the object of the `cast`
// leak at the `del` of its result, which stands on a line of its own. A `del` of a value that a
// `cast` of another function made, such as a parameter, gets no name.
fn name_bare_dels(named_vec mut* v, fir.func* f) void {
    bool mut@ own made = new(bool, cast(fir.local_count(f), u64));
    defer del(made);
    mark_cast_made(f, made);
    for (u32 mut b = 0; b < fir.block_count(f); b++) {
        fir.block* bl = fir.block_at(f, b);
        for (u64 mut k = 0; k < bl->statements.len; k++) {
            fir.statement* st = &bl->statements.items[k];
            bool from_cast = is_whole_local(st->place) && made[st->place.local];
            if (is_bare_del(f, st) && from_cast && st->loc.line < DEAD_LINE) {
                push_named(v, named_line{REPORT_LEAK, st->loc.line, f->sym});
            }
        }
    }
}

// Records the line that each line of the text `lines` names, for the report option `bit` and the
// function `f` of the report. A note of a site stands at the position of the site, so it names
// the line of the site again. A line of `--leak-report` at a `cast` names the line of the
// `cast`. When `f` holds that one `cast` (cast_count), the line names each `del` of `f` that only
// such a `cast` makes leak (name_bare_dels). In a function with two or more `cast` statements
// the line names no `del`: it cannot tell the `del` of the result of the reported `cast` from
// the `del` of the result of a `cast` that the pass proved.
fn name_lines(named_vec mut* v, u32 bit, fir.func* f, string lines) void {
    u64 mut start = 0;
    for (u64 mut k = 0; k < lines.len; k++) {
        if (lines[k] != '\n') {
            continue;
        }
        string text = lines[start..k];
        u32 line = named_of(text);
        if (line != 0) {
            push_named(v, named_line{bit, line, f->sym});
        }
        if (line != 0 && bit == REPORT_LEAK && str.ends_with(text, CAST_END)
            && cast_count(f) == 1) {
            name_bare_dels(v, f);
        }
        start = k + 1;
    }
}

/// Appends to `v` each line that fort.owner.report names for the proof `p` of `f`, with the bit
/// REPORT_LEAK for a line of `--leak-report` and REPORT_WHOLE for a line of
/// `--whole-store-report`, and gives the REPORT bits of the options that write a line for `f`.
/// The gate of the oracle tests and the typed judgement both take their report lines from here.
fn name_reports(check_env.env mut* c, named_vec mut* v, fir.func* f, prover.proof* p) u32 {
    strbuf.str_buf mut lines = strbuf.create();
    defer del(lines.data);
    report.write_inners(f, p, &c->m.s, &lines);
    u32 leak = lines.len > 0 ? REPORT_LEAK : 0;
    name_lines(v, REPORT_LEAK, f, strbuf.view(&lines));
    strbuf.clear(&lines);
    report.write_wholes(f, p, &c->m.s, &lines);
    name_lines(v, REPORT_WHOLE, f, strbuf.view(&lines));
    return leak | (lines.len > 0 ? REPORT_WHOLE : 0);
}

/// The REPORT bits of the report options with a line in `named` that names the line `line` of
/// the file `file` for an event of the function `of`. A file that is not the entry module
/// (is_entry) gets no bit. A line that a pass reports for a function names that function only: a
/// FIR text gives two functions the same lines. A line of `--span-del-report` has no function and
/// names its line for each. An event on a line of a reported function that no report line names
/// gets no bit, so `rule` gives it no report ruling. The test compares the line and not the
/// column: two sites on one line of the text are one site here.
fn reports_at(named_line@ named, ast.sym* of, string file, u32 line) u32 {
    u32 mut bits = 0;
    for (u64 mut k = 0; is_entry(file) && k < named.len; k++) {
        if (named[k].line == line && (named[k].of == null || named[k].of == of)) {
            bits = bits | named[k].bit;
        }
    }
    return bits;
}

/// The line of the entry module of the `del(s, d)` at `i` of the checker of `c` when the pass
/// refuses it, or 0: its drop `d` is not release-only by `drops` (D17.9). The gate of the oracle
/// tests and the typed judgement both give the function on that line an error.
fn refused_drain(check_env.env mut* c, release.table* drops, u64 i) u32 {
    ast.node* d = ast.ast_child(check.drain_at(&c->ck, i), 1);
    if (release.is_release_only(drops, d->sym) || !is_entry(d->loc.file)) {
        return 0;
    }
    return d->loc.line;
}

// Gives each item its verdict, as overwrite_entry of fort.driver does with each report option:
// the proof of each function of the entry module, then the release-only bit of the drop of each
// `del(s, d)`, then each `del` of a span of owners with one operand.
fn prove_batch(check_env.env mut* c, batch mut* bt) void {
    release.table mut* own drops = release.make();
    defer release.drop_made(move(drops));
    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < bt->m.funcs.len; i++) {
        fir.func* f = &bt->m.funcs.items[i];
        release.scan(drops, f);
        if (is_in_main(f->sym)) {
            prove_one(c, bt, f);
        }
    }
    release.solve(drops);
    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < check.drain_count(&c->ck); i++) {
        u32 line = refused_drain(c, drops, i);
        if (line != 0) {
            mark(bt, line, verdict.vd_rejected);
        }
    }
    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < check.span_del_count(&c->ck); i++) {
        diag.loc at = check.span_del_at(&c->ck, i)->a->loc;
        if (is_entry(at.file)) {
            mark(bt, at.line, verdict.vd_reported);
            push_named(&bt->named, named_line{REPORT_SPAN, at.line, null});
        }
    }
}

// Records whether the block of a `dead` marker ends in an abort.
fn push_abort(batch mut* bt, bool abort) void {
    if (bt->deads == bt->aborts.len) {
        bool mut@ own bigger = new(bool, fir.grown_capacity(bt->deads, "judgement: capacity"));
        for (u64 mut i = 0; i < bt->deads; i++) {
            bigger[i] = bt->aborts[i];
        }
        del(bt->aborts);
        bt->aborts = move(bigger);
    }
    bt->aborts[bt->deads] = abort;
    bt->deads += 1;
}

// Moves each `dead` marker of each function of `bt` to its own line, DEAD_LINE and its position
// in bt->aborts. The run gives the `leak` event of a `dead` at its location, so the line names the
// marker. A `dead` changes no fact and no run, so the move changes no verdict and no other event.
fn relocate(batch mut* bt) void {
    bt->deads = 0;
    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < bt->m.funcs.len; i++) {
        fir.func mut* f = &bt->m.funcs.items[i];
        for (u32 mut b = 0; b < fir.block_count(f); b++) {
            fir.block mut* bl = fir.block_mut(f, b);
            for (u64 mut k = 0; k < bl->statements.len; k++) {
                fir.statement mut* st = &bl->statements.items[k];
                if (st->kind != fir.stmt_kind.st_dead) {
                    continue;
                }
                push_abort(bt, fir.term_is_abort(&bl->term));
                st->loc.line = DEAD_LINE + cast(bt->deads - 1, u32);
                st->loc.end_line = st->loc.line;
            }
        }
    }
}

// Appends `add` to `v`.
fn push_failure(failure_vec mut* v, failure add) void {
    if (v->len == v->items.len) {
        failure mut@ own bigger = new(failure, fir.grown_capacity(v->len, "judgement: capacity"));
        for (u64 mut i = 0; i < v->len; i++) {
            bigger[i] = v->items[i];
        }
        del(v->items);
        v->items = move(bigger);
    }
    v->items[v->len] = add;
    v->len += 1;
}

// The place of an error of a run of the item `it` of `bt` on the line `line`: the row of
// generator.OPERATIONS of the statement on that line, or AT_END, AT_SCOPE or AT_CALLEE. `inside`
// says that the error stands in the function of the item. A `dead` marker of a declaration of
// the batch is in a callee.
fn place_of(batch* bt, item* it, bool inside, u32 line) u64 {
    u64 pos = statement_of(bt, it, inside, line);
    if (!inside) {
        return AT_CALLEE;
    } else if (line >= DEAD_LINE) {
        return AT_SCOPE;
    } else if (pos < bt->n) {
        return it->shape.ops[pos];
    }
    return AT_END;
}

// The statement of the item `it` of `bt` on the line `line`, or `bt->n` when the line holds no
// statement of it or `inside` is false.
fn statement_of(batch* bt, item* it, bool inside, u32 line) u64 {
    u32 start = it->first + it->shape.first;
    if (inside && line >= start && cast(line - start, u64) < bt->n) {
        return cast(line - start, u64);
    }
    return bt->n;
}

/// Whether `line` is the line of a `dead` marker after relocate whose block ends in an abort.
/// `aborts` holds one element for each marker, in the order of the lines from DEAD_LINE.
fn at_abort(bool@ aborts, u32 line) bool {
    return line >= DEAD_LINE && cast(line - DEAD_LINE, u64) < aborts.len
        && aborts[cast(line - DEAD_LINE, u64)];
}

/// Whether the disposition table trusts the kind `kind` (disposition.TABLE): an event of that
/// kind needs a program that breaks an assumption of the rules. `rule` takes such an event as an
/// error.
fn is_trusted(shadow.event_kind kind) bool {
    return disposition.TABLE[disposition.index_of(kind)].disposition
        == disposition.disposition_kind.dk_trusted;
}

/// The ruling of the event `ev` of a run of a function with exit 0. `at_abort` says that the
/// event stands at a `dead` marker of a block that ends in an abort, and `reports` holds the
/// REPORT bits of the options with a report line that names the line of the event (reports_at).
/// The event goes through the disposition table first (disposition.is_error), which reads its
/// kind, its cycle mark and `at_abort`. The table takes a `leak` at an abort (D17.20) and a `leak
/// at exit` of a cycle of owners as no error. An event of a kind that the table trusts is an
/// error here, so each kind is an error unless a rule takes the event: one of those two, or a
/// line of the report option that owns the kind, on the line of the event. `--leak-report` and
/// `--span-del-report` own `leak`, and `--whole-store-report` owns `overwrite`. No option owns
/// another kind. A `leak at exit` stands at the last statement of its run and not at the
/// statement that lost the allocation, so no report line names it and no option owns it.
fn rule(shadow.event* ev, bool at_abort, u32 reports) ruling {
    shadow.event_kind kind = ev->kind;
    bool leak = kind == shadow.event_kind.ev_leak;
    bool error = is_trusted(kind) || disposition.is_error(ev, at_abort);
    if (!error) {
        return kind == shadow.event_kind.ev_leak_at_exit ? ruling.ru_cycle : ruling.ru_excused;
    } else if (leak && (reports & REPORT_SPAN) != 0) {
        return ruling.ru_span_report;
    } else if (leak && (reports & REPORT_LEAK) != 0) {
        return ruling.ru_leak_report;
    } else if (kind == shadow.event_kind.ev_overwrite && (reports & REPORT_WHOLE) != 0) {
        return ruling.ru_whole_report;
    }
    return ruling.ru_error;
}

/// The ruling of the event `ev` of a run of a function with exit 0, in a batch with the `dead`
/// markers `aborts` (at_abort) and the report lines `named` (reports_at). The gate of the oracle
/// tests and the typed judgement both take the ruling of each event from here, so one rule judges
/// each family.
fn ruling_of(shadow.event* ev, bool@ aborts, named_line@ named) ruling {
    u32 reports = reports_at(named, ev->sym, ev->at.file, ev->at.line);
    return rule(ev, at_abort(aborts, ev->at.line), reports);
}

/// The row of END_TEXT of a run that ended with the outcome kind `kind` and, for a fault, the
/// text `what` of the fault.
fn end_of(interpreter.outcome_kind kind, string what) u64 {
    if (kind != interpreter.outcome_kind.oc_fault) {
        return cast(cast(kind, i32), u64);
    } else if (what == "null dereference") {
        return END_NULL;
    } else if (what == "invalid access") {
        return END_ACCESS;
    }
    return END_FAULT;
}

/// Whether a run of a function with exit 0 that ended in row `end` of END_TEXT with `errors`
/// error events is an error of its own: it ends in a fault other than `null dereference`, and no
/// error event explains the fault. A `null dereference` is the defined end of a read through
/// null. The ends `exit`, `trap` and `outside` are never an error of their own: no function of
/// the typed family has one of them, and the judgement has no rule for them.
fn is_silent(u64 end, u64 errors) bool {
    return (end == END_ACCESS || end == END_FAULT) && errors == 0;
}

// Makes the item `it` of `bt` a failure with the first error `what` at the place `at` and the
// statement `pos`, when it is none yet. A later error adds its kind to the record of the item.
fn fail(batch* bt, item mut* it, u64 what, u64 at, u64 pos, totals mut* t) void {
    u64 bit = 1;
    if (it->failed) {
        t->failures.items[it->record].kinds = t->failures.items[it->record].kinds | (bit << what);
        return;
    }
    it->failed = true;
    it->record = t->failures.len;
    failure mut f = failure{};
    f.ty = bt->ty;
    f.st = bt->st;
    f.n = bt->n;
    f.seq = it->seq;
    f.said = it->said;
    f.what = what;
    f.at = at;
    f.pos = pos;
    f.kinds = bit << what;
    f.ops = it->shape.ops;
    push_failure(&t->failures, f);
}

// Counts the run `run` of item `k` and its events. For a function with exit 0 it gives each event
// its ruling and counts the end of the run; the first error makes the item a failure.
fn collect(batch mut* bt, u64 k, interpreter.machine* run, totals mut* t) void {
    item mut* it = &bt->items[k];
    t->runs += 1;
    if (run->end.kind == interpreter.outcome_kind.oc_none) {
        panic("judgement.collect: a run did not end");
    }
    bool judged = it->said == verdict.vd_accepted || it->said == verdict.vd_reported;
    u64 mut errors = 0;
    for (u64 mut e = 0; e < run->sh.events.len; e++) {
        shadow.event* ev = &run->sh.events.items[e];
        u64 kind = cast(cast(ev->kind, i32), u64);
        t->events[kind] += 1;
        if (!judged) {
            continue;
        }
        ruling said = ruling_of(ev, bt->aborts[..bt->deads], bt->named.items[..bt->named.len]);
        t->rulings[cast(cast(said, i32), u64)] += 1;
        if (said == ruling.ru_error) {
            bool inside = ev->sym == it->sym;
            t->errors[kind] += 1;
            errors += 1;
            fail(bt, it, kind, place_of(bt, it, inside, ev->at.line),
                 statement_of(bt, it, inside, ev->at.line), t);
        }
    }
    // `errors` counts the error events only. A run tells this count from the count of each event
    // only when it ends in a fault that is_silent reads, holds an event with another ruling and
    // holds no error event. Some runs of the 3-operation family end in such a fault after an event
    // with another ruling, and each of them holds an error event too. So no test holds this choice.
    if (judged) {
        collect_end(bt, it, &run->end, errors, t);
    }
}

// Counts the end `end` of a run of the item `it`, a function with exit 0, with `errors` error
// events. A fault with no error event makes the item a failure (is_silent).
fn collect_end(batch* bt, item mut* it, interpreter.outcome* end, u64 errors,
               totals mut* t) void {
    u64 row = end_of(end->kind, end->what);
    t->judged += 1;
    t->ends[row] += 1;
    if (is_silent(row, errors)) {
        bool inside = is_entry(end->at.file) && it->first <= end->at.line
            && end->at.line <= it->last;
        t->silent += 1;
        fail(bt, it, row - END_ACCESS + WHAT_ACCESS, place_of(bt, it, inside, end->at.line),
             statement_of(bt, it, inside, end->at.line), t);
    }
}

// Runs item `k` with the input `input`: bit 0 is the `bool`, bit 1 the index, and bit 2 says that
// the place holds an owner at the entry.
fn run_drive(check_env.env mut* c, batch mut* bt, u64 k, u64 input, totals mut* t) void {
    interpreter.machine mut* own run = interpreter.make(&c->ck, &bt->m, true);
    defer interpreter.drop_made(move(run));
    u64[4] mut args = {};
    args[0] = interpreter.code_address(run, bt->items[k].sym);
    for (u64 mut bit = 0; bit < 3; bit++) {
        args[bit + 1] = (input >> bit) & 1;
    }
    interpreter.run_function(run, check_env.sym_main(c, generator.DRIVE), args[..]);
    collect(bt, k, run, t);
}

// Whether the input `input` of run_drive changes only what a function with the inputs `reads`
// does not read.
fn is_unread(u64 input, u32 reads) bool {
    return ((input & 1) != 0 && (reads & generator.READS_B) == 0)
        || ((input & 2) != 0 && (reads & generator.READS_I) == 0)
        || ((input & 4) != 0 && (reads & generator.READS_LIVE) == 0);
}

// Runs each item of `bt` that is not refused, with each input that it reads, and counts each item
// by its verdict.
fn run_batch(check_env.env mut* c, batch mut* bt, totals mut* t) void {
    for (u64 mut k = 0; k < bt->items.len; k++) {
        verdict said = bt->items[k].said;
        u32 reads = bt->items[k].shape.reads;
        t->functions += 1;
        t->verdicts[cast(cast(said, i32), u64)] += 1;
        if (said != verdict.vd_refused && bt->items[k].sym == null) {
            panic("judgement.run_batch: the batch holds no function for an item");
        }
        for (u64 mut input = 0; said != verdict.vd_refused && input < 8; input++) {
            if (!is_unread(input, reads)) {
                run_drive(c, bt, k, input, t);
            }
        }
    }
}

/// Judges the functions `first` to `first + count - 1` of `n` operations of the cell of row `ty`
/// of generator.TYPES and row `st` of generator.STORAGES, as one batch, and adds its counts to
/// `t`.
fn judge_batch(check_env.env mut* c, u64 ty, u64 st, u64 n, u64 first, u64 count,
               totals mut* t) void {
    batch mut* own bt = batch_make(ty, st, n, first, count);
    defer batch_drop_made(move(bt));
    string@ none = {};
    prepare(c, bt, "", none);
    judge_prepared(c, bt, t);
}

// Gives each item of the batch `bt` after prepare its verdict, counts the lines that its report
// lines name, moves each `dead` marker to its own line, runs each item, and adds the counts to `t`.
fn judge_prepared(check_env.env mut* c, batch mut* bt, totals mut* t) void {
    prove_batch(c, bt);
    for (u64 mut k = 0; k < bt->named.len; k++) {
        for (u64 mut bit = 0; bit < REPORTS; bit++) {
            t->named[bit] += cast((bt->named.items[k].bit >> cast(bit, u32)) & 1, u64);
        }
    }
    relocate(bt);
    run_batch(c, bt, t);
}

/// Judges the functions `texts` as one batch, and adds its counts to `t`. `decls` holds the
/// declarations before them, and each text ends in a newline. A function has the parameters
/// `(bool b, u64 i)` and reads the inputs `reads`, as READS bits of generator. `decls` declares
/// `drive(fn (bool, u64) void f, bool b, u64 i, bool live) i32`, which calls `f`. A failure of
/// the batch has no cell and no statement. The test of the judgement itself calls it.
fn judge_hand(check_env.env mut* c, string decls, string@ texts, u32 reads, totals mut* t) void {
    batch mut* own bt = hand_make(texts.len, reads);
    defer batch_drop_made(move(bt));
    prepare(c, bt, decls, texts);
    judge_prepared(c, bt, t);
}

/// Judges the functions of `n` operations of the cell of row `ty` of generator.TYPES and row `st`
/// of generator.STORAGES, in batches of BATCH. `*turn` counts the batches of the family, and the
/// call judges the batches whose count is `part` modulo `parts`, so `parts` calls with each
/// `part` judge each function once.
fn judge_cell(check_env.env mut* c, u64 ty, u64 st, u64 n, u64 part, u64 parts, u64 mut* turn,
              totals mut* t) void {
    if (!generator.typed_fits(ty, st)) {
        return;
    }
    u64 total = generator.typed_cell_count(ty, st, n);
    for (u64 mut first = 0; first < total; first += BATCH) {
        u64 mut count = BATCH;
        if (first + count > total) {
            count = total - first;
        }
        if (*turn % parts == part) {
            judge_batch(c, ty, st, n, first, count, t);
        }
        *turn += 1;
    }
}

/// Judges part `part` of `parts` of the functions of `n` operations of the typed family: each cell
/// in the order of the tables.
fn judge_family(check_env.env mut* c, u64 n, u64 part, u64 parts, totals mut* t) void {
    u64 mut turn = 0;
    for (u64 mut ty = 0; ty < generator.TYPE_COUNT; ty++) {
        for (u64 mut st = 0; st < generator.STORAGE_COUNT; st++) {
            judge_cell(c, ty, st, n, part, parts, &turn, t);
        }
    }
}

// The text of the place `at` of an error.
fn place_text(u64 at) string {
    if (at < generator.OPERATION_COUNT) {
        return generator.OPERATIONS[at].name;
    }
    return PLACE_TEXT[at - generator.OPERATION_COUNT];
}

// The text of the first error `what` of a failure.
fn what_text(u64 what) string {
    if (what < KINDS) {
        return shadow.EVENT_TEXT[what];
    }
    return END_TEXT[what - WHAT_ACCESS + END_ACCESS];
}

/// A class of the known failures: a key, which no change of a table moves, and the cause.
struct class_row {
    string key;
    string cause;
}

/// The count of the rows of CLASSES, and the class of a failure with no class.
u64 CLASS_COUNT = 8;
u64 NO_CLASS = CLASS_COUNT;

/// The rows of CLASSES.
u64 CLASS_MOVED = 0;
u64 CLASS_SPAN_POINTER = 1;
u64 CLASS_RANGE_CALLBACK = 2;
u64 CLASS_VIEW = 3;
u64 CLASS_MOVED_VALUE = 4;
u64 CLASS_RELEASED = 5;
u64 CLASS_SCOPE = 6;
u64 CLASS_CALLEE_READ = 7;

/// The classes of the known failures (class_of). The use rule gives an owner that is no local no
/// obligation (D17.21), and no rule holds a pointer into an `own` span, a view of an allocation
/// or the collection of a range loop across a callback: the run time takes those four classes.
/// It takes the class `callee read` too: the case X2 of spec/ownership-design.md 3.4, a callee
/// that reads the vacated slot of a local of its caller through a pointer. The slot holds the zero
/// value (D17.6), and the design gives the case a runtime error (D10.6). The operation `callee
/// read` of the typed family reaches it (is_callee_read), and so does an item of the gate
/// (class_of_event). The classes `released` and `scope` are the classes of an item of the gate of
/// the oracle tests only, and no function of the typed family gets one of them.
/// The class `moved value` has no owner yet: a `move` of a vacated place gives the zero value and
/// a store ends the vacated state of its target (D17.21), and fir.shadow gives the event at the
/// read of the place that took that value. The 2-operation family reaches the classes `moved`,
/// `span pointer`, `range callback` and `callee read`; functions of 3 operations reach each of
/// the six. No
/// class takes a `del` of the collection in the body of its range loop: the checker refuses that
/// function (D17.10), so such a failure is a new one.
class_row[CLASS_COUNT] CLASSES = {
    {"moved", "a use of an owner that is no local after its move or del"},
    {"span pointer", "a use through a pointer into an own span after del(s, d) of the span"},
    {"range callback", "a callback that releases the collection of a range loop"},
    {"view", "a load through a view after the release of the allocation that it points to"},
    {"moved value", "a read of a place that holds the value of the move of a vacated place"},
    {"released", "an item of the gate that uses an allocation after its release"},
    {"scope", "an item of the gate that uses a local after the end of its scope"},
    {"callee read", "a callee reads the vacated slot of a local of its caller through a pointer"},
};

/// The class of an item of the gate of the oracle tests whose first error is the event `ev`, or
/// NO_CLASS. Such an item is a program of a hand case, of the seeded family or of the shapes. It
/// has no row of generator.OPERATIONS, so its class reads the event only: a narrower test
/// (class_of) needs the names of the statements. A `use after move` reads the moved tag of the
/// address of the access (fir.shadow): `sym` names the function whose frame held the vacated
/// slot, or is null when no frame held it, and `flag` says that another frame read the slot. A
/// slot of no frame is an owner that is no local (`moved`). A slot of a frame that another frame
/// read is the case X2 (`callee read`). A function that reads a vacated slot of its own frame
/// breaks rule (b) of D17.21, so that failure has no class. The count of each class of each unit
/// holds the list exactly (KNOWN), so a new item stops the test although its event has a class.
fn class_of_event(shadow.event* ev) u64 {
    shadow.origin moved = ev->t.f;
    bool tagged = moved.kind == shadow.tag_kind.tg_moved;
    if (ev->kind == shadow.event_kind.ev_use_after_move && tagged && moved.sym == null) {
        return CLASS_MOVED;
    } else if (ev->kind == shadow.event_kind.ev_use_after_move && tagged && moved.flag) {
        return CLASS_CALLEE_READ;
    } else if (ev->kind == shadow.event_kind.ev_use_after_release) {
        return CLASS_RELEASED;
    } else if (ev->kind == shadow.event_kind.ev_use_after_scope) {
        return CLASS_SCOPE;
    }
    return NO_CLASS;
}

/// Counts the failing item of the gate of the oracle tests whose first error is the event `ev`
/// into `by`: the count of the failing items of each class of CLASSES, then of those with no class
/// (class_of_event). hold_known reads `by`.
fn count_failure(u64 mut@ by, shadow.event* ev) void {
    by[class_of_event(ev)] += 1;
}

// Whether row `op` of generator.OPERATIONS has the name `name`. A place that is no statement has
// no name.
fn is_op(u64 op, string name) bool {
    return op < generator.OPERATION_COUNT && generator.OPERATIONS[op].name == name;
}

// Whether a statement of `f` from statement `from` has the name `name`.
fn holds(failure* f, u64 from, string name) bool {
    for (u64 mut k = from; k < f->n; k++) {
        if (is_op(f->ops[k], name)) {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}

// Whether a statement of `f` before statement `to` has the name `name`.
fn holds_before(failure* f, u64 to, string name) bool {
    for (u64 mut k = 0; k < to && k < f->n; k++) {
        if (is_op(f->ops[k], name)) {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}

/// The count of the rows of VACATES.
u64 VACATE_COUNT = 14;

/// The operations whose statement moves or releases the whole place and does not fill it again
/// on each path. `swap` is no row: it fills the place before its end. `self` vacates the place
/// only in a storage class that passes `move` of the place to the call (is_vacate), and the drop
/// of `defer` runs at the end of its block (is_vacated).
string[VACATE_COUNT] VACATES = {
    "release", "move out", "drop", "defer", "self", "while", "cast", "switch arms", "shallow",
    "release through", "move through", "to global", "take", "else",
};

// Whether the statement of row `op` of generator.OPERATIONS vacates the whole place of `f`: a row
// of VACATES, and for `self` a storage class whose call of the function moves the place.
fn is_vacate(failure* f, u64 op) bool {
    if (is_op(op, "self") && str.find(generator.STORAGES[f->st].again, "move(") < 0) {
        return false;
    }
    for (u64 mut k = 0; k < VACATE_COUNT; k++) {
        if (is_op(op, VACATES[k])) {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}

/// The count of the rows of FILLS.
u64 FILL_COUNT = 6;

/// The operations whose statement stores a whole value into the place on each path. A store ends
/// the vacated state of its target (D17.21), so a use after such a statement is no use of a
/// vacated place. `else` and `switch arms` store on one path only and are rows of VACATES.
/// `regrow` is no row: a place that it can follow vacated stays vacated, which rejects more.
string[FILL_COUNT] FILLS = {"alloc", "clear", "move in", "choose", "from global", "swap"};

// Whether the statement of row `op` of generator.OPERATIONS is a row of FILLS.
fn is_fill(u64 op) bool {
    for (u64 mut k = 0; k < FILL_COUNT; k++) {
        if (is_op(op, FILLS[k])) {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}

// Whether a statement of `f` from statement `from` and before statement `to` fills the place.
fn is_filled(failure* f, u64 from, u64 to) bool {
    for (u64 mut k = from; k < to && k < f->n; k++) {
        if (is_fill(f->ops[k])) {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}

// Whether a loop opens before statement `to` of `f`. The block of a loop closes after the last
// statement, so the loop holds each later statement, and each of them runs again in a later turn.
fn is_turned(failure* f, u64 to) bool {
    return holds_before(f, to, "loop") || holds_before(f, to, "range");
}

// Whether a loop holds the statement of the error of `f`. Each statement of such a loop can run
// before the error in a later turn. A loop that opens at the statement of the error holds the
// error too: the head of a range loop reads its collection in each turn.
fn is_error_turned(failure* f) bool {
    return is_turned(f, f->pos + 1);
}

// Whether the statement of the error of `f` tests the whole place against `null` before each use
// of it: `choose load` in a type whose test is `%P != null`. The edge where such a test finds a
// value that is not `null` ends the vacated state (D17.21), and the load stands on that edge.
fn is_tested(failure* f) bool {
    return is_op(f->at, "choose load") && generator.TYPES[f->ty].holds == "%P != null";
}

// Whether the place of `f` can be vacated at the use of the error, in the sense of D17.21: a
// statement that vacates the whole place can run before the error, and no event that ends the
// state follows it. The events are a store into the place (FILLS) and the test of is_tested. A
// statement before the error counts, unless it is a `defer`, whose drop runs at the end of its
// block, or a later statement before the error fills the place. A `defer` counts in a later turn
// of a loop that opens before it. A statement that is not before the error counts in a later turn
// of a loop that holds the error (is_error_turned). The test reads no fill in those two cases.
fn is_vacated(failure* f) bool {
    if (is_tested(f)) {
        return false;
    }
    for (u64 mut k = 0; k < f->n; k++) {
        bool before = k < f->pos;
        bool direct = before && !is_op(f->ops[k], "defer");
        if (!is_vacate(f, f->ops[k])) {
            continue;
        } else if (direct && !is_filled(f, k + 1, f->pos)) {
            return true;
        } else if (!direct && (before ? is_turned(f, k) : is_error_turned(f))) {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}

/// Whether the text `text` of a row of generator.OPERATIONS moves or releases an inner owner of
/// the place: it holds a `move` or a `del` whose operand starts with the code of an inner owner,
/// `%S` or `%O`. No row of OPERATIONS holds the term `move(%O`. The term stays for a new row of
/// that shape, and the test of the judgement holds it with a text of its own.
fn is_part_vacate_text(string text) bool {
    return str.find(text, "move(%S") >= 0 || str.find(text, "del(%S") >= 0
        || str.find(text, "move(%O") >= 0 || str.find(text, "del(%O") >= 0;
}

/// Whether the statement of row `op` of generator.OPERATIONS moves or releases an inner owner of
/// the place (is_part_vacate_text). The test reads the text of the row and no list of names, so
/// a new row of that shape needs no change here. A place that is no statement has no text.
fn is_part_vacate(u64 op) bool {
    return op < generator.OPERATION_COUNT
        && is_part_vacate_text(generator.OPERATIONS[op].text);
}

// Whether a statement of `f` from statement `from` and before statement `to` moves or releases
// an inner owner of the place (is_part_vacate).
fn holds_part(failure* f, u64 from, u64 to) bool {
    for (u64 mut k = from; k < to && k < f->n; k++) {
        if (is_part_vacate(f->ops[k])) {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}

// Whether a statement that moves or releases an inner owner of the place of `f` can run before
// the error. A statement before the error counts. A statement that is not before the error
// counts in a later turn of a loop that holds the error (is_error_turned): the body of a range
// loop can write an element of its collection (D17.10). The test reads no event that ends the
// vacated state of the inner owner (D17.21): it is true also when a store into the place
// follows the statement.
fn is_part_vacated(failure* f) bool {
    return holds_part(f, 0, is_error_turned(f) ? f->n : f->pos);
}

// Whether the owner that a statement of `f` uses after its move can be no local. The place of
// the storage classes `field`, `spanned` and `global` is no local, so each use after move in
// those three is such a use: the test reads no statement there. In each other storage class
// the place is a local, and an owner behind it is no local: the types `box` and `span` hold their
// inner owners behind a pointer, and the function must move or release one of them. A place that
// can be vacated at the error (is_vacated) gives the use a second cause, the move of the local,
// which the use rule must reject: that failure has no class.
fn is_far(failure* f) bool {
    string st = generator.STORAGES[f->st].name;
    string ty = generator.TYPES[f->ty].name;
    if (st == "field" || st == "spanned" || st == "global") {
        return true;
    }
    return (ty == "box" || ty == "span") && !is_vacated(f)
        && holds_part(f, 0, f->n);
}

// The failure `f` with its error at statement `k` in place of a callee: the statement `callee
// read` whose callee gave the error.
fn callee_read_at(failure* f, u64 k) failure {
    failure mut at = *f;
    at.at = f->ops[k];
    at.pos = k;
    return at;
}

// Whether the error of `at`, a statement `callee read` (generator.PEEK), is the case X2: the
// storage class keeps the place in a local of the function, and a slot of the local can be
// vacated at the statement. The slot is the whole place (is_vacated), or an inner owner of a type
// that holds its inner owners in the place and not behind a pointer as `box` and `span` do
// (is_part_vacated). The callee reads the vacated slot of a local of its caller through the
// pointer that the statement passes.
fn is_callee_read(failure* at) bool {
    string st = generator.STORAGES[at->st].name;
    string ty = generator.TYPES[at->ty].name;
    if (st == "field" || st == "spanned" || st == "global") {
        return false;
    }
    bool in_place = ty != "box" && ty != "span";
    return is_vacated(at) || (in_place && is_part_vacated(at));
}

// The class of the failure `f`, a `use after move` in a callee of a function with a statement
// `callee read`, or NO_CLASS. The callee of such a statement reads through `&%P`, so the test
// reads each such statement as the place of the error (callee_read_at), in the order of the
// statements, and takes the first that agrees with a cause: an owner that is no local
// (`moved`, is_far), the case X2 (`callee read`, is_callee_read), or a place that holds the value
// of the `move` of a vacated place (`moved value`, is_moved_value). A `self` after the statement
// passes such a value to the function itself, and the callee of that call reads it.
fn class_of_callee_read(failure* f) u64 {
    for (u64 mut k = 0; k < f->n; k++) {
        failure at = callee_read_at(f, k);
        if (!is_op(f->ops[k], "callee read")) {
            continue;
        } else if (is_far(&at)) {
            return CLASS_MOVED;
        } else if (is_callee_read(&at)) {
            return CLASS_CALLEE_READ;
        } else if (is_moved_value(&at)) {
            return CLASS_MOVED_VALUE;
        }
    }
    return NO_CLASS;
}

// Whether the error of `f` stands at a use through the pointer `p` into the `own` span of the
// storage class `spanned`, in a function that releases the span with `del(s, d)`.
fn is_span_pointer(failure* f) bool {
    bool through = is_op(f->at, "store through") || is_op(f->at, "release through")
        || is_op(f->at, "move through");
    return through && generator.STORAGES[f->st].name == "spanned" && holds(f, 0, "container");
}

// Whether a statement of `f` before the error gives the view `v` a pointer.
fn is_viewed(failure* f) bool {
    return holds_before(f, f->pos, "lend") || holds_before(f, f->pos, "lend call")
        || holds_before(f, f->pos, "extern pass");
}

// Whether a statement of `f` after the error passes `move` of the place to the function itself:
// a `self` in a storage class whose call of the function moves the place (is_vacate). The call
// of each other storage class passes no value of the place, so its `self` is no cause.
fn is_passed(failure* f) bool {
    for (u64 mut k = f->pos + 1; k < f->n; k++) {
        if (is_op(f->ops[k], "self") && is_vacate(f, f->ops[k])) {
            return true;
        }
    }
    return false;
}

// Whether a statement of `f` before the error runs while the whole place can be vacated: it
// follows a statement that vacates the whole place, no statement between the two fills the place,
// and it is no fill itself. A `defer` vacates at the end of its block and is no such statement.
// The use rule must reject each use of a local owner in that state (D17.21). The test holds no
// list of the statements that use the place, so each statement there counts, also one that the
// rule permits, such as a second `move`. So the test is true for more functions than the rule
// rejects, and each of them has no class.
fn is_between(failure* f) bool {
    for (u64 mut j = 1; j < f->pos && j < f->n; j++) {
        if (is_fill(f->ops[j])) {
            continue;
        }
        for (u64 mut k = 0; k < j; k++) {
            if (is_vacate(f, f->ops[k]) && !is_op(f->ops[k], "defer")
                && !is_filled(f, k + 1, j)) {
                return true;
            }
        }
    }
    return false;
}

// Whether the place that the error of `f` reads can hold the value of the `move` of a vacated
// place, and the use has no other cause. The cause: a statement before the error stores
// `move(y)` into the place, or a statement after it passes `move` of the place to the function
// itself (is_passed), and the error stands in that call. `else` and `switch arms` store
// `move(y)` on one path and release the place on the other one, so they are rows of VACATES and
// no store here. Another cause is a statement that can run before the error and vacates the
// whole place (is_vacated) or an inner owner of it (is_part_vacated). The inner owners of this
// class stand in the fixed storage of the local, because is_far takes the types that hold them
// behind a pointer. So the use rule must reject the use after each of the two (D17.21), and
// that failure has no class. A third cause is a statement that runs while the whole place can be
// vacated, before the store that ends the state (is_between): the compiler must reject the
// function at that statement when it uses the place, so the function shows a loss of the use
// rule and has no class. A function of 4 operations is the first with such a statement before a
// store and a use.
fn is_moved_value(failure* f) bool {
    u64 pos = f->pos;
    bool stored = holds_before(f, pos, "move in") || holds_before(f, pos, "choose")
        || holds_before(f, pos, "swap");
    return !is_vacated(f) && !is_part_vacated(f) && !is_between(f) && (is_passed(f) || stored);
}

/// The row of CLASSES of the failure `f`, or NO_CLASS. A class takes a failure only when the
/// kind of the first error, its place and the names of the statements agree with the cause of the
/// class. class_of reads the names and no run, so the test is necessary for the cause and does
/// not prove it. It reads the first error only: a second kind of error of the function shows in
/// the line of the failure (append_kinds) and changes no class.
fn class_of(failure* f) u64 {
    bool moved = f->what == cast(cast(shadow.event_kind.ev_use_after_move, i32), u64);
    bool released = f->what == cast(cast(shadow.event_kind.ev_use_after_release, i32), u64);
    bool at_range = released && is_op(f->at, "range");
    if (moved && f->at == AT_CALLEE && holds(f, 0, "callee read")) {
        return class_of_callee_read(f);
    } else if (moved && is_far(f)) {
        return CLASS_MOVED;
    } else if (released && is_span_pointer(f)) {
        return CLASS_SPAN_POINTER;
    } else if (at_range && holds(f, f->pos + 1, "extern call")
               && generator.STORAGES[f->st].shared.len > 0) {
        return CLASS_RANGE_CALLBACK;
    } else if (released && is_op(f->at, "load") && is_viewed(f)) {
        return CLASS_VIEW;
    } else if (moved && is_moved_value(f)) {
        return CLASS_MOVED_VALUE;
    }
    return NO_CLASS;
}

/// The count of the failures of `t` with the class `class`, a row of CLASSES or NO_CLASS.
fn class_count(totals* t, u64 class) u64 {
    u64 mut count = 0;
    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < t->failures.len; i++) {
        if (class_of(&t->failures.items[i]) == class) {
            count += 1;
        }
    }
    return count;
}

// The key of the class `class`.
fn class_key(u64 class) string {
    if (class < CLASS_COUNT) {
        return CLASSES[class].key;
    }
    return "none";
}

/// The units of KNOWN. The first three are the sets of the gate of the oracle tests
/// (test/fort/owner/oracle_test.ft): the hand items with the FIR cases, the seeded family and the
/// shapes. Then the parts of the typed family of FAMILY operations: part `k` of PARTS, from 1, is
/// the unit UNIT_TYPED + k - 1.
u64 UNIT_HAND = 0;
u64 UNIT_SEEDED = 1;
u64 UNIT_SHAPES = 2;
u64 UNIT_TYPED = 3;

/// A row of KNOWN: `count` failures of the unit `unit` have the class `class` of CLASSES.
struct known_row {
    u64 unit;
    u64 class;
    u64 count;
}

/// The count of the rows of KNOWN.
u64 KNOWN_ROWS = 29;

/// The known failures of the closed-world judgement, as one list for each family: the count of
/// the failures of each unit by class, each a measured count. A unit and a class with no row have
/// the count 0. A unit must give each count exactly (holds_known). So a count goes down only, and
/// in the change that makes its function pass or makes the compiler refuse it. A count goes up
/// only with an edit of this list. The expected output of each test names each failure: the key
/// of a function of the typed family, and the line `fails the gate` of an item of the gate.
known_row[KNOWN_ROWS] KNOWN = {
    {UNIT_HAND, CLASS_CALLEE_READ, 1},
    {UNIT_HAND, CLASS_RELEASED, 11},
    {UNIT_HAND, CLASS_SCOPE, 2},
    {UNIT_SEEDED, CLASS_MOVED, 1},
    {UNIT_SEEDED, CLASS_RELEASED, 96},
    {UNIT_SHAPES, CLASS_RELEASED, 18},
    {UNIT_TYPED, CLASS_MOVED, 97},
    {UNIT_TYPED, CLASS_RANGE_CALLBACK, 1},
    {UNIT_TYPED, CLASS_CALLEE_READ, 4},
    {UNIT_TYPED + 1, CLASS_MOVED, 90},
    {UNIT_TYPED + 1, CLASS_CALLEE_READ, 4},
    {UNIT_TYPED + 2, CLASS_MOVED, 95},
    {UNIT_TYPED + 2, CLASS_CALLEE_READ, 4},
    {UNIT_TYPED + 3, CLASS_MOVED, 55},
    {UNIT_TYPED + 3, CLASS_SPAN_POINTER, 2},
    {UNIT_TYPED + 3, CLASS_CALLEE_READ, 2},
    {UNIT_TYPED + 4, CLASS_MOVED, 50},
    {UNIT_TYPED + 4, CLASS_SPAN_POINTER, 3},
    {UNIT_TYPED + 4, CLASS_RANGE_CALLBACK, 2},
    {UNIT_TYPED + 4, CLASS_CALLEE_READ, 8},
    {UNIT_TYPED + 5, CLASS_MOVED, 47},
    {UNIT_TYPED + 5, CLASS_CALLEE_READ, 8},
    {UNIT_TYPED + 6, CLASS_MOVED, 73},
    {UNIT_TYPED + 6, CLASS_SPAN_POINTER, 5},
    {UNIT_TYPED + 6, CLASS_CALLEE_READ, 6},
    {UNIT_TYPED + 7, CLASS_MOVED, 78},
    {UNIT_TYPED + 7, CLASS_SPAN_POINTER, 3},
    {UNIT_TYPED + 7, CLASS_RANGE_CALLBACK, 1},
    {UNIT_TYPED + 7, CLASS_CALLEE_READ, 6},
};

/// The count of the failures of the class `class` that KNOWN lists for the unit `unit`.
fn known_count(u64 unit, u64 class) u64 {
    u64 mut count = 0;
    for (u64 mut r = 0; r < KNOWN.len; r++) {
        if (KNOWN[r].unit == unit && KNOWN[r].class == class) {
            count += KNOWN[r].count;
        }
    }
    return count;
}

/// The count of the failures of the class `class` that KNOWN lists for each unit.
fn known_of_class(u64 class) u64 {
    u64 mut count = 0;
    for (u64 mut r = 0; r < KNOWN.len; r++) {
        if (KNOWN[r].class == class) {
            count += KNOWN[r].count;
        }
    }
    return count;
}

/// Holds the failures of the unit `unit` against KNOWN, and gives whether the unit gives the
/// list exactly. `counts` holds the count of its failures of each class of CLASSES, then the count
/// of its failures with no class. The unit must have no failure with no class, and the count that
/// KNOWN lists for each class. It prints one line under `name` for each count that differs, and
/// then the count of the failures that KNOWN lists for the unit. More failures than the list are
/// new failures. Fewer mean that a function passes now: its count goes down in the same change.
fn holds_known(string name, u64 unit, u64@ counts) bool {
    u64 mut listed = 0;
    bool mut held = counts[NO_CLASS] == 0;
    if (!held) {
        println(name, ": failures with no class ", counts[NO_CLASS], ": each is a new failure");
    }
    for (u64 mut class = 0; class < CLASS_COUNT; class++) {
        u64 expected = known_count(unit, class);
        listed += expected;
        if (counts[class] > expected) {
            println(name, ": class ", class_key(class), ": failures ", counts[class],
                    ", KNOWN lists ", expected, ": a failure is new");
            held = false;
        } else if (counts[class] < expected) {
            println(name, ": class ", class_key(class), ": failures ", counts[class],
                    ", KNOWN lists ", expected, ": lower the count of KNOWN");
            held = false;
        }
    }
    println(name, ": failures in KNOWN ", listed);
    return held;
}

/// Stops the test when the failures `counts` of the unit `unit` do not give KNOWN exactly
/// (holds_known). The gate of the oracle tests holds each of its three sets here.
fn hold_known(string name, u64 unit, u64@ counts) void {
    if (!holds_known(name, unit, counts)) {
        panic("judgement.hold_known: the failures of a unit are not those that KNOWN lists");
    }
}

/// Appends the key of the function of `f`: the names of its type, of its storage class and of
/// each operation. A new row of a table changes the key of no function.
fn append_key(strbuf.str_buf mut* b, failure* f) void {
    strbuf.append(b, generator.TYPES[f->ty].name);
    strbuf.push(b, '/');
    strbuf.append(b, generator.STORAGES[f->st].name);
    strbuf.push(b, ':');
    for (u64 mut k = 0; k < f->n; k++) {
        if (k > 0) {
            strbuf.push(b, ';');
        }
        strbuf.push(b, ' ');
        strbuf.append(b, generator.OPERATIONS[f->ops[k]].name);
    }
}

/// Appends `, also <kind>` for each kind of error of the failure `f` other than its first error,
/// in the order of shadow.event_kind and then `invalid access` and `other fault`.
fn append_kinds(strbuf.str_buf mut* b, failure* f) void {
    for (u64 mut what = 0; what < KINDS + 2; what++) {
        if (what != f->what && ((f->kinds >> what) & 1) != 0) {
            strbuf.append(b, ", also ");
            strbuf.append(b, what_text(what));
        }
    }
}

/// Prints the counts of `t` under `name`: the functions by verdict; the runs; the runs of the
/// functions with exit 0 by end; the events of each run by kind, also of a rejected function;
/// then, for the functions with exit 0, the error events by kind, the error events of a kind
/// that the disposition table does not trust and those of a kind that it trusts, the events by
/// each other ruling, and the failures.
fn print_totals(string name, totals* t) void {
    strbuf.str_buf mut b = strbuf.create();
    defer del(b.data);
    u64 mut others = 0;
    u64 mut trusted = 0;
    for (u64 mut kind = 0; kind < KINDS; kind++) {
        if (disposition.TABLE[kind].disposition == disposition.disposition_kind.dk_trusted) {
            trusted += t->errors[kind];
        } else {
            others += t->errors[kind];
        }
    }
    append_verdicts(&b, t);
    println(name, strbuf.view(&b));
    println(name, ": runs ", t->runs, ", of the functions with exit 0 ", t->judged);
    print_counts(name, ": ends: ", END_TEXT[1..], t->ends[1..], 5);
    print_counts(name, ": events: ", shadow.EVENT_TEXT[..], t->events[..], 4);
    print_counts(name, ": errors: ", shadow.EVENT_TEXT[..], t->errors[..], 4);
    println(name, ": errors of a trusted kind ", trusted, ", of another kind ", others);
    print_counts(name, ": not errors: ", RULING_TEXT[1..], t->rulings[1..], 2);
    println(name, ": faults with no error event ", t->silent, ", failures ", t->failures.len);
}

// Appends the count of the functions of `t`, then the count of each verdict after its name.
fn append_verdicts(strbuf.str_buf mut* b, totals* t) void {
    strbuf.append(b, ": functions ");
    strbuf.append_u64(b, t->functions);
    for (u64 mut k = 0; k < VERDICTS; k++) {
        strbuf.append(b, ", ");
        strbuf.append(b, VERDICT_TEXT[k]);
        strbuf.push(b, ' ');
        strbuf.append_u64(b, t->verdicts[k]);
    }
}

// Prints each element of `counts` after its text of `texts`, after `name` and `head`, with
// `width` counts on a line.
fn print_counts(string name, string head, string@ texts, u64@ counts, u64 width) void {
    strbuf.str_buf mut b = strbuf.create();
    defer del(b.data);
    for (u64 mut k = 0; k < counts.len; k++) {
        strbuf.append(&b, texts[k]);
        strbuf.push(&b, ' ');
        strbuf.append_u64(&b, counts[k]);
        if ((k + 1) % width == 0 || k + 1 == counts.len) {
            println(name, head, strbuf.view(&b));
            strbuf.clear(&b);
        } else {
            strbuf.append(&b, ", ");
        }
    }
}

/// Prints the count of the failures of `t` of each class of CLASSES under `name`, then the count
/// of the failures with no class.
fn print_classes(string name, totals* t) void {
    for (u64 mut class = 0; class <= CLASS_COUNT; class++) {
        println(name, ": class ", class_key(class), ": ", class_count(t, class));
    }
}

/// Prints two lines for each failure of `t` under `name`: the key of the function, then its
/// class, what its first error is and the place of it, each other kind of error of its runs
/// (append_kinds), and its verdict.
fn print_failures(string name, totals* t) void {
    strbuf.str_buf mut b = strbuf.create();
    defer del(b.data);
    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < t->failures.len; i++) {
        failure* f = &t->failures.items[i];
        strbuf.clear(&b);
        append_key(&b, f);
        println(name, ": failure: ", strbuf.view(&b));
        strbuf.clear(&b);
        append_kinds(&b, f);
        println(name, ":     ", class_key(class_of(f)), ": ", what_text(f->what), " at ",
                place_text(f->at), strbuf.view(&b), ": ",
                VERDICT_TEXT[cast(cast(f->said, i32), u64)]);
    }
}

/// Appends the text of the function of the failure `f`, after a line with its key.
fn write_failure(strbuf.str_buf mut* b, failure* f) void {
    u64[generator.OPERATION_COUNT] mut ops = {};
    u64 size = generator.typed_vocabulary(f->ty, f->st, ops[..]);
    generator.typed_shape mut shape = generator.typed_shape{};
    strbuf.append(b, "// ");
    append_key(b, f);
    strbuf.push(b, '\n');
    generator.write_typed(b, f->ty, f->st, ops[..size], f->n, f->seq, f->seq, &shape);
}

// Prints the first function of each class of the failures of `t`, the one with the lowest cell
// and index, and each function with no class.
fn print_examples(totals* t) void {
    for (u64 mut i = 0; i < t->failures.len; i++) {
        failure* f = &t->failures.items[i];
        u64 class = class_of(f);
        bool mut first = true;
        for (u64 mut k = 0; k < i; k++) {
            if (class != NO_CLASS && class_of(&t->failures.items[k]) == class) {
                first = false;
            }
        }
        if (first) {
            print_failure(f);
        }
    }
}

// Prints the function of the failure `f` after its class, its first error, the place of it and
// each other kind of error of its runs.
fn print_failure(failure* f) void {
    strbuf.str_buf mut b = strbuf.create();
    defer del(b.data);
    strbuf.append(&b, "// class ");
    strbuf.append(&b, class_key(class_of(f)));
    strbuf.append(&b, ": ");
    strbuf.append(&b, what_text(f->what));
    strbuf.append(&b, " at ");
    strbuf.append(&b, place_text(f->at));
    append_kinds(&b, f);
    strbuf.push(&b, '\n');
    write_failure(&b, f);
    print(strbuf.view(&b));
}

/// The count of the operations of each function of the family that PR CI judges, and the count of
/// the parts that PR CI judges it in.
u64 FAMILY = 2;
u64 PARTS = 8;

/// The main function of the test of part `part` of PARTS, from 1. With no argument it judges that
/// part of the family of FAMILY operations, and prints its counts, the count of its failures of
/// each class, and two lines for each failure. The expected output of the test is the list of the
/// known failures of the part: a function that leaves the list, one that enters it and one that
/// changes its class each change a line. With the arguments `<operations> <part> <parts>` it
/// judges that part of that family, and prints the text of the first function of each class and
/// of each function with no class after those lines. With no argument it gives 0 only when the
/// part gives the counts of KNOWN exactly (known_exit). With the arguments it gives 1 when a
/// failure has no class, and 0 when each failure has a class of CLASSES (exit_of).
fn run_part(u64 part, string@ args) i32 {
    u64 mut n = FAMILY;
    u64 mut k = part;
    u64 mut parts = PARTS;
    bool chosen = args.len > 1;
    if (chosen && !read_choice(args, &n, &k, &parts)) {
        eprintln("usage: <test> [<operations> <part> <parts>]");
        return 2;
    }
    check_env.env mut* own c = check_env.make();
    defer check_env.drop_made(move(c));
    totals mut* own t = make();
    defer drop_made(move(t));
    println("typed: operations ", n, ", part ", k, " of ", parts);
    judge_family(c, n, k - 1, parts, t);
    print_totals("typed", t);
    print_classes("typed", t);
    print_failures("typed", t);
    if (chosen) {
        print_examples(t);
        return exit_of(t);
    }
    return known_exit("typed", UNIT_TYPED + part - 1, t);
}

/// The exit status of run_part for the counts `t` of a family that KNOWN does not list: 1 when a
/// failure has no class.
fn exit_of(totals* t) i32 {
    if (class_count(t, NO_CLASS) > 0) {
        return 1;
    }
    return 0;
}

/// The exit status of run_part for the counts `t` of the unit `unit` of KNOWN: 0 when the unit
/// gives its list exactly (holds_known), and 1 when it does not.
fn known_exit(string name, u64 unit, totals* t) i32 {
    u64[CLASS_COUNT + 1] mut counts = {};
    for (u64 mut class = 0; class <= CLASS_COUNT; class++) {
        counts[class] = class_count(t, class);
    }
    if (holds_known(name, unit, counts[..])) {
        return 0;
    }
    return 1;
}

// Reads the three arguments of run_part, and gives whether they name a part of a family.
fn read_choice(string@ args, u64 mut* n, u64 mut* part, u64 mut* parts) bool {
    return args.len == 4 && str.parse_u64(args[1], 10, n) && str.parse_u64(args[2], 10, part)
        && str.parse_u64(args[3], 10, parts) && *n <= generator.MOST_STATEMENTS && *part >= 1
        && *part <= *parts;
}

/// Judges the functions of `n` operations of each cell apart, and prints one line for each cell:
/// the size of its vocabulary, its functions by verdict and its failures. A cell whose
/// declarations the compiler does not accept stops the test, and a cell that accepts no function
/// shows in its line.
fn print_cells(u64 n) void {
    check_env.env mut* own c = check_env.make();
    defer check_env.drop_made(move(c));
    for (u64 mut ty = 0; ty < generator.TYPE_COUNT; ty++) {
        for (u64 mut st = 0; st < generator.STORAGE_COUNT; st++) {
            if (generator.typed_fits(ty, st)) {
                print_cell(c, ty, st, n);
            }
        }
    }
}

// Judges the functions of `n` operations of one cell, and prints its line.
fn print_cell(check_env.env mut* c, u64 ty, u64 st, u64 n) void {
    totals mut* own t = make();
    defer drop_made(move(t));
    u64 mut turn = 0;
    judge_cell(c, ty, st, n, 0, 1, &turn, t);
    print_cell_line(ty, st, t);
}

// Prints the line of the cell with the counts `t`. The buffer has a frame of its own, so no call
// stands between its release and the end of the frame.
fn print_cell_line(u64 ty, u64 st, totals* t) void {
    strbuf.str_buf mut b = strbuf.create();
    defer del(b.data);
    append_verdicts(&b, t);
    println(generator.TYPES[ty].name, "/", generator.STORAGES[st].name, strbuf.view(&b),
            ", failures ", t->failures.len);
}
